Do not modify, redraw, regenerate, duplicate, rename, or replace any PNG. The existing tests/charged-ba2.test.mjs intentionally verifies the exact uploaded bytes.

The important correction is that 0001_rasen12.png is a release/recovery frame. It is not part of the actual rush/contact animation.

Intended move

Charged BA2 is a committed forward rush with the blue sphere. If the sphere catches an opponent, the opponent is held by the spinning sphere until it explodes. If the rush catches nobody, #0001 still needs to visibly perform the final release/recovery frame before returning to neutral.

The move should read visually as:

1. sphere forms;
2. #0001 rushes forward;
3. either:
    * miss -> release/recovery frame -> neutral, or
    * hit -> enemy immediately shows Hurt -> sphere stays attached and visibly rotates -> delayed explosion -> #0001 uses the release frame;
4. move ends.

Correct the rasen fighter animation split

The current code defines:

* rasenForm = rasen1-3
* rasenDash = rasen4-6
* rasenConfirm = rasen7-12

That last split is wrong because rasen12 is being treated as part of the confirm sequence and then held for almost the entire explosion delay.

Change the animation registration so rasen12 is isolated as a dedicated release animation.

The intended structural split should be along these lines after visually confirming the frames:

* rasenForm: rasen1-rasen3
* rasenDash: rasen4-rasen6
* rasenConfirm: rasen7-rasen11
* rasenRelease: only rasen12

rasenRelease should be a one-shot, one-frame fighter animation at RASEN_FPS.

Do not leave rasen12 inside rasenConfirm.

Update comments/specification accordingly.

Clean whiff behavior

This is currently definitely wrong.

At the moment, ChargedTechnique.update() returns 'miss' as soon as the dash animation completes. Fighter.endTechnique('miss') then destroys the technique immediately, removes the sphere, and returns #0001 directly to idle.

The existing test even explicitly expects:

“no rasen7-12 … idle at once”

That behavior must be replaced.

When the forward rush finishes without hitting anyone:

* stop horizontal rush movement;
* stop contact searching;
* do not deal any damage;
* do not bind anyone;
* do not play the impact or explosion sphere frames;
* transition into a short release/recovery phase;
* show 0001_rasen12.png;
* do not return to idle on the same simulation step;
* keep control locked for the release frame’s intended duration, normally one RASEN_FPS frame (1 / 12 s);
* after that release frame completes, finish the technique and return control.

The blue sphere does not explode on a miss. This is a release/recovery of the failed rush, not an explosion.

The final visible fighter frame of a clean miss must therefore be 0001_rasen12.png, followed by the normal idle/run state.

Keep velocity at zero during this release.

A wall-ending rush should not accidentally continue sliding. Preserve existing wall/ground safety behavior. Do not invent new combat effects for a whiff.

Successful contact

When the rushing sphere overlaps an opponent:

* first hit happens exactly once;
* preserve the existing first-hit damage unless another existing design source says otherwise: currently 4 damage;
* stop #0001’s rush immediately;
* move the sphere onto the opponent;
* bind/immobilize the opponent;
* begin the contact/confirm fighter frames;
* start the delayed explosion timer;
* there must be no additional rush contact checks after confirmation.

The existing targetOffset, sphere hitbox, facing snapshot, grounding requirement and bind ownership model are useful and should be retained.

Enemy must show Hurt immediately on sphere contact

This needs to happen on the exact simulation step on which the sphere first damages the target.

Currently CombatSystem.applyHit() gives the victim hitstun, but charged-technique collision resolution happens after both fighters have already updated their visual state for that fixed step. As a result, the target can render its previous animation for the initial collision frame.

Fix that.

Immediately after the sphere’s first hit is successfully applied/bound, force the caught target’s visual state to reflect the new combat state before that step is rendered.

For a grounded target, the rendered fighter frame on the contact step must be:

0001_hurt.png

For an airborne target, use the existing:

0001_midairhurt.png

The victim should remain on the appropriate Hurt pose while caught/bound, as the current hitstun / bound animation rules already intend.

Prefer a targeted solution for the technique if possible instead of casually changing timing semantics for every attack in the game. If you decide the correct fix belongs in shared hit handling, run all combat tests and make sure ordinary attacks, Dodge, hitstop, projectiles and clones are unchanged.

Add a regression test that checks the target frame immediately after the contact tick, not one tick later.

The sphere must rotate continuously while attached to the enemy

This is another current bug.

Right now:

* rasenSphereImpact contains prasen7, prasen8, prasen9;
* ChargedTechnique.sphere uses frameAt(...);
* frameAt() clamps to the final frame;
* therefore it plays 7 -> 8 -> 9 once and then freezes permanently on prasen9 until the explosion.

That is not correct.

The attached blue sphere is supposed to visibly keep rotating on the enemy until it explodes.

Use the authored prasen7, prasen8, prasen9 frames as the rotating cycle:

prasen7 -> prasen8 -> prasen9 -> prasen7 -> prasen8 -> prasen9 -> ...

Continue at the appropriate PRASEN_FPS for the entire bound/wait period.

Do not just hold prasen9.

Do not rotate the entire canvas sprite artificially if these three PNG frames are themselves the authored rotational animation. Use the actual animation frames.

Implementation can either:

* make the impact effect explicitly looping and teach the charged-technique frame selection to honor looping; or
* use a dedicated modulo-based frame calculation for the attached-sphere phase.

Whichever approach is cleaner, ensure the frame progression is deterministic at the fixed 60 Hz simulation rate.

The sphere center must continue following the caught opponent using the existing targetOffset.

Explosion

The last two sphere frames are the explosion.

Keep:

* prasen10
* prasen11

as the two-frame rasenSphereExplosion one-shot.

These are visually lighter/brighter because this is when the actual blast happens. They must not participate in the attached rotation loop.

So the effect sequence on a successful hit should be:

prasen7 -> 8 -> 9 -> 7 -> 8 -> 9 -> ... repeatedly

until the explosion time, then:

prasen10 -> prasen11

once.

The second hit should still occur on the first explosion frame (prasen10) unless inspection of existing design/tests establishes a deliberate different timing.

Preserve the existing important ordering:

1. explosion becomes due;
2. release the target from the bind;
3. apply explosion damage/knockback;
4. target can then be launched normally.

Do not let the bind cancel the explosion knockback.

Current values can remain:

* first hit: 4 damage
* explosion: 16 damage
* total successful unblocked damage: 20
* explosionDelay: 2.0 seconds

unless there is an authoritative existing value elsewhere in the repository.

Use rasen12 as the actual release frame

This is the central animation correction.

rasen12 should not be sitting on screen for almost two seconds while the sphere is attached to the opponent.

During the wait before explosion, #0001 should hold the final appropriate pre-release/contact pose from the real contact sequence — after inspecting the art, this will most likely mean the last frame of the new rasenConfirm (rasen11).

Then when the sphere is released/explodes, transition #0001 to:

0001_rasen12.png

That is the release frame.

Use the same release artwork when the rush completely misses.

In other words, rasen12 represents leaving/releasing the technique, not continuing the actual rush.

A good phase model would be something like:

* form
* dash
* confirm
* wait
* explode
* missRelease or a generic release
* done

You do not have to use those exact names if there is a cleaner state model, but the distinction must exist behaviorally.

For a hit:

* form
* dash
* confirm (rasen7-rasen11)
* wait while holding the correct contact pose and continuously rotating prasen7-9
* explosion starts
* fighter displays rasen12 as the release
* sphere displays prasen10-11
* finish

For a miss:

* form
* dash
* no target found
* fighter displays rasen12 for its release/recovery duration
* no impact/explosion sphere sequence
* finish

